WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.220
By the time a movie hits your screen,&nbsp;
every frame has been examined by dozens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.300
if not hundreds, of eyes. Still,&nbsp;
anachronisms and magical color&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:13.740
changing shirts do make their way in to&nbsp;
films. Here are some of our favorites.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:21.060
Judging by its 16 screen adaptations, Pride and&nbsp;
Prejudice is arguably Jane Austen's most beloved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:25.500
work. It's an archetypal rom-com about&nbsp;
the cross-class romance between proper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:30.900
Mr. Darcy and fiercely independent Elizabeth&nbsp;
Bennet. Keira Knightley received an Academy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:37.020
Award nomination for playing Elizabeth in the&nbsp;
2005 incarnation. Director Joe Wright set his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:43.020
take on Pride and Prejudice in 1797, around the&nbsp;
time Austen started work on the original novel.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:48.780
The movie strives for historical authenticity, but&nbsp;
fell short in the footwear department. Elizabeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:54.720
is seen traipsing around a garden in rubber boots,&nbsp;
or Wellingtons as they're called in the U.K. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:00.120
Wellies weren't a thing in Britain until the&nbsp;
1850s making Elizabeth's boots an anachronism.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:05.160
Former teen journalist Cameron Crowe&nbsp;
won a screenwriting Oscar for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:10.980
semi-autobiographical movie Almost Famous.&nbsp;
Patrick Fugit plays 15-year-old William Miller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:16.680
a precociously talented music writer hired&nbsp;
to review a Black Sabbath concert. Backstage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:21.960
he meets the members of Stillwater, the fictional&nbsp;
band he tours with for the rest of the movie. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:26.460
one point, William watches the band members argue&nbsp;
about the design of their first-ever t-shirt.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.840
"Gentlemen, your first t-shirts have arrived."

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.200
Then, later in the film, Stillwater makes a&nbsp;
stop in Kansas where the lead singer takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:42.000
William to a house party. There's a fan of&nbsp;
the band there, wearing a Stillwater T-shirt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.100
and it's not the design the musicians were&nbsp;
discussing earlier, for its first and only shirt.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:55.020
Between 1973's American Graffiti, the 1974&nbsp;
debut of Happy Days, and 1978's Grease,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.020 --> 00:02:01.140
the 1970s were full of nostalgia for the&nbsp;
1950s. Grease was an absolute blockbuster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:05.880
becoming the highest-grossing film of the&nbsp;
year. But despite all the poodle skirts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:10.680
malt shops, and slicked-back hair,&nbsp;
it was very much a 70s film, and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:13.620
touch of the contemporary showed&nbsp;
in some of the costume choices.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:20.040
During a pivotal dance scene, Danny Zuko, played&nbsp;
by John Travolta, wears some quintessentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:27.420
1970s threads that no teenager would or could&nbsp;
have worn in the 1950s — namely a big-collared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:33.060
pink dress shirt under a leisure suit. A few&nbsp;
moments later, at the climax of "Born to Hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:39.240
Jive," Marty Maraschino, played by Dinah Manoff,&nbsp;
slides across the floor, exposing an accessory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:43.740
worn to protect the actor which was not a&nbsp;
fashion choice for the character: a kneepad.

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:49.560
Unforgiven, winner of the 1992 Academy&nbsp;
Award for Best Picture, is one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:55.620
bleakest Westerns ever made. It offers a gritty,&nbsp;
unsettling look at life in the West in the 1880s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:01.680
in contrast to most mid-20th century Westerns&nbsp;
with their clear heroes and villains and rosy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:07.320
adventurous spirit. In Unforgiven, former&nbsp;
criminal William Munny, played by Clint Eastwood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:12.720
descends on a tiny Wyoming town in search of two&nbsp;
violent cowboys with a bounty on their heads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.820
much to the chagrin of anti-vigilante&nbsp;
sheriff Little Bill Daggett. Daggett&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:21.900
holds his pants up with a belt threaded&nbsp;
through sewn-on belt loops. Pants with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:26.100
built-in belt loops were available before&nbsp;
the 20th century, but were very rare.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:30.540
The Levi Strauss Company didn't begin&nbsp;
attaching belt loops until the 1920s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:35.760
In the late 19th century, when Unforgiven is&nbsp;
set, cowboys and other denizens of the American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.800
west wore suspenders. Or else they wore pants&nbsp;
that fit tightly enough that they stayed put.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:46.440
In the Godfather films, Francis Ford Coppola&nbsp;
tells an epic tale about the Corleones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:51.840
a Sicilian-American family who figures large&nbsp;
in the organized criminal underworld of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:58.080
early to mid-20th century. The first Godfather is&nbsp;
set primarily in the 1940s, and filmmakers paid a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:03.120
lot of attention to authenticity, capturing the&nbsp;
look and clothing of World War II-era America.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:08.100
But it's not easy to shoot on the streets,&nbsp;
and mistakes happen. In one sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:13.260
a vintage car pulls up to a 1940s-looking&nbsp;
Las Vegas hotel. In the background stand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:17.820
a couple of onlookers — not actors.&nbsp;
They're the very picture of late '60s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:21.660
to early '70s middle-class America,&nbsp;
down to their bushy hairstyles.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:28.260
For decades, Jackie Chan has dominated the global&nbsp;
box office with his martial arts-based action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:34.980
movies. In 2001, Chan produced and starred in the&nbsp;
action comedy The Accidental Spy. And nowhere does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:39.480
Chan's signature style present more than in&nbsp;
a lengthy fight scene in which his character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:44.040
Buck Yuen, an exercise gear salesman&nbsp;
turned criminal organization target,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:49.380
fights off one bad guy after another while&nbsp;
completely nude. It's comical how Chan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:54.660
as Yuen, attempts to keep himself covered with&nbsp;
whatever random objects are at hand. Pinpoint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.660 --> 00:05:00.240
precise cinematography also helps keep the&nbsp;
nudity PG-13. But the sequence isn't perfect.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:02.700
"Accidents do happen."

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:08.340
Here and there, Chan gives the audience a little&nbsp;
bit more of himself than intended. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:15.000
in one way or another, The Accidental Spy&nbsp;
accidentally exposes almost all of Jackie Chan.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:21.240
Dirty Dancing was a massive hit in 1987.&nbsp;
Viewers of all ages were charmed by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:25.140
story of a summer romance between&nbsp;
17-year-old Frances "Baby" Houseman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:28.380
played by Jennifer Grey,&nbsp;
and dance instructor Johnny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:34.020
played by Patrick Swayze. Dirty Dancing reminded&nbsp;
older audiences of the good old days when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:39.480
took summer vacations at Catskills resorts&nbsp;
and sex talk was for science class, if that.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:44.040
While keeping things as expressly&nbsp;
early '60s as possible, Dirty Dancing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:49.140
filmmakers made a major misstep when it&nbsp;
came to Baby's wardrobe. She spends most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:53.040
of the summer in a pair of very short&nbsp;
denim shorts. Not only would those be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:59.100
considered risqué according to early 1960s&nbsp;
standards, but they didn't even exist. Denim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:03.780
shorts like Baby's didn't go mainstream&nbsp;
until the much more libertine '70s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:09.600
when they were popularized by – and named after –&nbsp;
Daisy Duke, a character on The Dukes of Hazzard.

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:15.240
The Indiana Jones movies were conceived as&nbsp;
an homage to mid-century film serials by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:21.120
franchise creators Steven Spielberg and George&nbsp;
Lucas. Fictional treasure-hunting archaeologist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:27.300
Indiana Jones is the hero, and in Raiders of the&nbsp;
Lost Ark and Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:35.040
Nazis are the bad guys. Last Crusade is set in&nbsp;
1938, and Indy encounters Nazis at a book burning.

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:42.360
"Nazis. I hate these guys."

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:48.720
The Nazis are clearly identifiable by their&nbsp;
uniforms, which are all real. They were sourced in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:54.240
Eastern Europe by the film's co-costume designer&nbsp;
Joanna Johnston. But they aren't completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.240 --> 00:07:01.080
faithful to 1938. All the actors portraying Nazis&nbsp;
wear uniforms loaded with medals awarded for tasks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:06.540
and acts of war committed during World War II.&nbsp;
The thing is, that war took place largely in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:12.120
the 1940s. So those medals, and the battles in&nbsp;
which they'd be won, wouldn't have happened yet.

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:15.900
Braveheart is the true-life&nbsp;
story of William Wallace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:21.180
a 13th-century Scottish folk hero and leader&nbsp;
of the opposition against the more powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:25.800
and better-equipped English forces. While&nbsp;
the characters and story are mostly true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:31.260
Braveheart's still a movie, and filmmakers —&nbsp;
including Mel Gibson, recipient of the Best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:36.000
Director and Best Picture Oscars for the film —&nbsp;
took a number of creative liberties with history.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:42.240
Braveheart deals with Scottish identity, and,&nbsp;
unfortunately, many of the film's inaccuracies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:47.400
relate to just that. For example, the&nbsp;
most enduring images from the film are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:52.680
of Gibson as the warrior Wallace heading into&nbsp;
battle wearing blue face paint, known as woad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:58.680
and a plaid kilt. The thing is: warriors wore woad&nbsp;
about 1,000 years before Braveheart takes place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:04.620
and real Scots didn't wear the type of tartan&nbsp;
that Gibson's Wallace wears until 500 years later.

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:08.520
Today, thoroughbred horse&nbsp;
racing is a specialty sport,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:13.680
but in the early to mid-1900s, it was&nbsp;
enormously popular. It takes a big-budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:18.660
high-profile Hollywood movie like the&nbsp;
2003 Seabiscuit to restore fame and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:23.340
interest to the forgotten athletes, horses&nbsp;
and riders, of the period. Seabiscuit was an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:27.300
unlikely champion who became a big winner and&nbsp;
crowd favorite during the Great Depression.

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:32.820
Tobey Maguire played Seabiscuit's primary&nbsp;
jockey, John "Red" Pollard. In the film's many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:38.640
racing scenes, he's seen wearing a racing helmet&nbsp;
with a prominent chin strap. But in the 1930s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:43.320
jockeys' helmets didn't have chin straps.&nbsp;
Those weren't introduced into horse racing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:48.420
until the 1950s, about 20 years after&nbsp;
the events depicted in Seabiscuit.

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:54.780
Miloš Forman's film adaptation of Peter Shaffer's&nbsp;
play Amadeus tells the story of the last days of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:59.880
famous composer Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart,&nbsp;
and the hatred and envy felt by his rival,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:06.180
Antonio Salieri. In 1985, Amadeus won eight&nbsp;
Academy Awards, including Best Picture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:12.240
Best Director, Best Actor, and Best Costume&nbsp;
Design. The head of wardrobe, Theodor Pistek,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:16.680
brought the lush, elaborate world of&nbsp;
late 1700s high society Vienna to life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:21.180
outfitting the actors in exquisite&nbsp;
wigs, waistcoats, lace, frills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:26.640
and petticoats. But Pistek put zippers on the&nbsp;
costumes. In a scene depicting the ballet in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:32.820
Mozart's opera The Marriage of Figaro, telltale&nbsp;
metal teeth can be seen on the dancers' outfits.

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:36.660
"I don't understand. Is it modern?"

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:41.400
Zippers didn't exist in 18th century Austria. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:44.280
modern zipper wasn't a thing&nbsp;
until about 100 years later.

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:51.120
La La Land both does and doesn't capture the vibe&nbsp;
of life in modern-day Los Angeles. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:55.560
there are surprisingly few spontaneous&nbsp;
but expertly choreographed musical numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.560 --> 00:10:00.060
on the freeway despite the countless&nbsp;
hours Angelenos spend stuck in traffic.

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:05.100
At one point, Sebastian, the character&nbsp;
played by Ryan Gosling, and Mia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:09.480
played by Emma Stone, have a road&nbsp;
rage encounter. During this sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:15.420
jazz pianist Sebastian wears an appropriately&nbsp;
retro brown shirt. But in the next scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:19.320
he arrives at his apartment to see his&nbsp;
sister, Laura wearing a blue shirt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:26.040
despite not having had the opportunity or time&nbsp;
to change. After Laura takes off, Sebastian sits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:30.780
down at the piano, and he's once again dressed&nbsp;
in that brown shirt. It's not a magical shirt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:36.960
nor were scenes of Sebastian furiously switching&nbsp;
shirts cut out. It's a continuity error, which La&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:42.660
La Land director Damien Chazelle acknowledges in&nbsp;
a commentary track on the DVD version of the film.

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:49.380
On the whole, Captain America: The First Avenger&nbsp;
is a solid piece of entertainment. It elevated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.380 --> 00:10:54.240
star Chris Evans to superstardom, spawned two&nbsp;
sequels, and is one of the best-loved Marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.240 --> 00:11:00.660
movies in history. What we're saying is: It's hard&nbsp;
to pick this movie apart. But in the midst of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:05.760
the "pulpy action" and "pleasantly retro vibe"&nbsp;
the critics applaud in Captain America, there's an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:11.100
historical slip-up we can hardly believe made it&nbsp;
into the film. About an hour and a half in, during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:16.920
the train raid scene, Kenneth Choi's Jim Morita&nbsp;
is shown wearing a high-tech earpiece — what looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:22.860
to be a Bowman Communication military headset&nbsp;
— which definitely didn't exist during WWII.

